@Window editor
The window editor is used for editing some details of screens which are
only used for text windows and item windows. (For screens that are only
used to display a board, this info is not used.)

Use left/right arrows to move the cursor; hold shift to copy the current
cell to where the cursor will be moved to as well.

Set the command by using the letter keys. Use numbers 3 and 4 to set the
colour and parameter. You can also use 1 and 2 to set the colour to use
the primary or secondary colour of the cell in the screen instead of using
a fixed colour code.

F1 is used for editing window-specific flags.
!flag;Window flags

F5 will save the window info, and F6 will delete it (even if it was already
present, it will be deleted).

Command codes for text windows:

þ A = Always use the window character, in a link line, or outside of the
hard edge of a normal line, or outside of the text of a $ line.

þ C = A link indicator which is only displayed if the cursor is here,
otherwise it is blank.

þ K = Key code for a link. Uses the parameter as the character code if it
is a link with no key code.

þ L = A link indicator. Displayed if it is a link line.

þ P = A link indicator which is only displayed if a key code is present.
A blank space is displayed if it is a link without a key code.

þ S = Surrounds a $ text in a text window, on both sides.

þ T = The text of a link. The parameter character is used if the text of
the link is shorter than the window. The first character will be displayed
at the soft edge.

þ X = No effect; always use the default behaviour. Some of the commands are
only meaningful in some contexts; if used in a context where it is not
appropriate then it is treated as X.

If the parameter is character code 0 then it will use the default character
in the screen. Character code 255 also has some special meanings for some
commands; see the descriptions above.

Meaningful commands by line types in text windows:
  Normal   - - - - - - - X
  Center   A - - - - S - X
  Link     A C K L P - T X

:flag;Flags

þ Single ends: This is used in text windows and means that the default
characters will be used only for the lines immediately before the first
line and immediately after the last line. If not selected, then all of
the lines outside of the text will show the default characters.

þ Zero-based line numbers: Line number displays are zero-based if this is
selected, otherwise they are one-based.

þ Horizontal scrolling: Not currently implemented.

þ XOR color: In a item window, when multiple colour overrides are applied,
this defines how to combine them. If this is selected then XOR is used,
otherwise OR is used.

You can also define some colours in the flags menu. Any of these colours
which is not zero will be used in place of the colour defined in the screen
in some circumstances.

Colours for text windows are:

þ Normal text: Overrides the colour of normal text (which is not a link,
centered text, or label).

þ Link text: Overrides the colour of link text (a line with ! at the
beginning). This is overridden by the A, C, K, L, P, and T commands.

þ Center text: Overrides the colour text with $ at the beginning.

þ Label text: Overrides the colour of the heading text of a label (a line
with : at the beginning), and of the entire row that contains it.

